      "hints": ["Track each mini-grid separately. Then compare corresponding cells across the three grids within a frame; also follow the bottom arrow.","Compare matching cells within each frame. Does black mean either input is black, or exactly one? Check whether the bottom arrow controls a preliminary turn.","The left mini-grid turns anticlockwise by 90°, then 180°, then 270°, then 360° (a full turn), then 90° again; the turn increases by 90° each time modulo a full turn. the middle mini-grid turns 90° clockwise each frame. Predict the remaining colour/count or panel features before choosing."],
      "optionNotes": ["Wrong: left mini-grid is [white, black, white, white] in reading order; it should be [black, white, white, white] in reading order; bottom arrow is 90° clockwise from up; it should be 0° clockwise from up.","Correct: left mini-grid: [black, white, white, white] in reading order; middle mini-grid: [black, black, white, white] in reading order; right mini-grid: [black, black, white, white] in reading order; bottom arrow: 0° clockwise from up.","Wrong: middle mini-grid is [white, black, black, black] in reading order; it should be [black, black, white, white] in reading order; right mini-grid is [black, white, black, black] in reading order; it should be [black, black, white, white] in reading order.","Wrong: left mini-grid is [white, white, black, black] in reading order; it should be [black, white, white, white] in reading order; right mini-grid is [white, white, white, black] in reading order; it should be [black, black, white, white] in reading order.","Wrong: right mini-grid is [white, white, white, black] in reading order; it should be [black, black, white, white] in reading order."],
      "solution": ["The left mini-grid turns anticlockwise by 90°, then 180°, then 270°, then 360° (a full turn), then 90° again; the turn increases by 90° each time modulo a full turn. Frames 1–6, left mini-grid: [white, black, white, white] in reading order → [black, white, white, white] in reading order → [white, white, white, black] in reading order → [white, white, black, white] in reading order → [white, white, black, white] in reading order → [white, white, white, black] in reading order. Next: [black, white, white, white] in reading order.","the middle mini-grid turns 90° clockwise each frame. Frames 1–6, middle mini-grid: [white, white, black, black] in reading order → [black, white, black, white] in reading order → [black, black, white, white] in reading order → [white, black, white, black] in reading order → [white, white, black, black] in reading order → [black, white, black, white] in reading order. Next: [black, black, white, white] in reading order.","rotate the left grid clockwise by the bottom arrow's angle measured clockwise from up in THAT frame, then union it with the middle grid to obtain the right grid. Frames 1–6, right mini-grid: [white, white, black, black] in reading order → [black, white, black, white] in reading order → [black, black, white, black] in reading order → [black, black, white, black] in reading order → [white, black, black, black] in reading order → [black, black, black, white] in reading order. Next: [black, black, white, white] in reading order. A corresponding cell is black when at least one input is black, including when both are black.","the bottom arrow turns 90° clockwise each frame. Frames 1–6, bottom arrow: 180° clockwise from up → 270° clockwise from up → 0° clockwise from up → 90° clockwise from up → 180° clockwise from up → 270° clockwise from up. Next: 0° clockwise from up."],
      "explanation": "Four relationships apply: The left mini-grid turns anticlockwise by 90°, then 180°, then 270°, then 360° (a full turn), then 90° again; the turn increases by 90° each time modulo a full turn; the middle mini-grid turns 90° clockwise each frame; rotate the left grid clockwise by the bottom arrow's angle measured clockwise from up in THAT frame, then union it with the middle grid to obtain the right grid; the bottom arrow turns 90° clockwise each frame. Check every displayed transition; option similarity alone does not establish a rule.",
